  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Seasonably cooler this weekend into next week \nwith bulk of precipitation confined near and southeast of Lake \nErie\n\nFollowing one of the warmest starts to November on record in\ncentral PA, (see climate section) we'll make a trip on the\nweather coaster back to seasonably cooler temps this weekend \nthrough the upcoming work week (though a slight rebound in temps\nwill occur Sunday through Tuesday). \n\nA compact and seasonably strong closed upper low over eastern \nLake Erie this morning will move across southern New York and \nlift northeast away from the area by Sunday morning. This \nfeature accompanied by cool air aloft and mean west to NW flow \nwill bring scattered to numerous showers to start the first full\nweekend of November. \n\nWith Wet Bulb 0C temps between 0.5-1 kft AGL this morning across\nthe NW MTNS of PA and sfc temps in the mid 30s, the showers will\nbe mixed with (or fall as) wet snow INVOF of KBFD and the ridge\ntops of the Laurel Highlands. No accumulation is expected. Wet \nbulb Ts will rise back above 2 KFT AGL and stay there through \nthe middle of next week.\n\nToday will be noticeably cooler and feel rather chilly given \nhow warm it has been during the day over the past week or so.\n\nThe other noticeable facet to the daytime weather will be a rather\ngusty wind that will vary from southwest to northwest with the \npassage of several weak and moisture challenged cold fronts into\nthe middle of next weak. Wind gusts will top out in the mid 20s \nto low 30s (mph) and could even peak around 40 mph from the \nnorthwest on Monday. \n\nA more substantial long wave trough may attempt to carve out \nover the eastern U.S. by the end of the week. \n\nMedium range guidance is coming into decent agreement for the \npotential for the first accumulating snow of the season \nThursday night into Friday (across mainly the higher terrain of\nNorthern and Western PA) as a deep area of low pressure and \nvery cold air aloft moves over the region with a developing \nsurface low along the Mid Atlantic Coast.",
